WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.170 --> 00:00:03.490
- How is it possible that modern
medicine recommends certain

00:00:03.490 --> 00:00:05.860
treatments that are flat out wrong.

00:00:05.860 --> 00:00:08.010
Well, that's what I asked Dr. Paul Offit,

00:00:08.010 --> 00:00:10.230
the director of Vaccine Education Center

00:00:10.230 --> 00:00:11.900
at Children's Hospital, Philadelphia

00:00:11.900 --> 00:00:14.670
and member of the FDA
Vaccine Advisory Committee.

00:00:14.670 --> 00:00:17.890
Yeah, he advises the FDA on
what vaccines to approve.

00:00:17.890 --> 00:00:20.700
He's not just an advisor
though, but a vaccine creator,

00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:22.910
having developed a rotavirus vaccine,

00:00:22.910 --> 00:00:25.860
which is estimated to save
hundreds of lives per day

00:00:25.860 --> 00:00:26.950
around the world.

00:00:26.950 --> 00:00:29.547
Dr. Offit is also the author
of one of my favorite books,

00:00:29.547 --> 00:00:32.140
"Overkill When Modern
Medicine Goes Too Far,"

00:00:32.140 --> 00:00:35.550
which directly challenges
15 modern medical practices,

00:00:35.550 --> 00:00:37.500
some of which you may
have experienced yourself.

00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:40.710
Huge thanks to Audible
for sponsoring this video.

00:00:40.710 --> 00:00:43.750
To start off Dr. Offit and I
discuss how important it is

00:00:43.750 --> 00:00:47.470
to be skeptical, even with
modern or revolutionary medicine.

00:00:47.470 --> 00:00:48.303
For example,

00:00:48.303 --> 00:00:52.090
he attacks the established
practice of lowering every fever

00:00:52.090 --> 00:00:53.860
and the overuse of antibiotics.

00:00:53.860 --> 00:00:56.380
So I asked him what's one to do,

00:00:56.380 --> 00:00:59.200
like for me as a young
family medicine physician,

00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:03.460
like let's take the antibiotics
discussion on its own.

00:01:03.460 --> 00:01:08.190
Your proponent for decreasing
lengths of antibiotic courses,

00:01:08.190 --> 00:01:10.800
when appropriate, decreasing
the use of antibiotics,

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:12.113
when not appropriate,

00:01:13.050 --> 00:01:17.010
it's easier said then done
then what is available in

00:01:17.010 --> 00:01:18.080
clinical practice.

00:01:18.080 --> 00:01:20.630
Patients come in with a certain
understanding that takes

00:01:20.630 --> 00:01:22.090
a lot of time to debunk,

00:01:22.090 --> 00:01:24.620
time that we oftentimes
don't have with the patients.

00:01:24.620 --> 00:01:28.770
A lot of times they come in
with sort of expectations that

00:01:28.770 --> 00:01:29.890
they should get the antibiotic.

00:01:29.890 --> 00:01:30.723
And if they don't,

00:01:30.723 --> 00:01:33.700
they will leave you a negative
review or negative score

00:01:33.700 --> 00:01:35.430
on your after visit summaries.

00:01:35.430 --> 00:01:38.390
And these are things that
active clinicians have

00:01:38.390 --> 00:01:40.430
to think about in addition to the science.

00:01:40.430 --> 00:01:42.750
And in addition to the worry
about what could potentially

00:01:42.750 --> 00:01:43.970
go wrong with this patient.

00:01:43.970 --> 00:01:46.130
What's your recommendation
to young doctors

00:01:46.130 --> 00:01:46.963
around the world?

00:01:46.963 --> 00:01:49.260
- Well, first of all, and I
would extend it to the hospital.

00:01:49.260 --> 00:01:51.310
I mean, I don't see outpatients,

00:01:51.310 --> 00:01:54.530
but I see inpatients in our
hospital and the physicians that

00:01:54.530 --> 00:01:57.410
are taking care of these
patients want antibiotics.

00:01:57.410 --> 00:01:58.970
They want them for a long period of time.

00:01:58.970 --> 00:02:01.030
So the pressure doesn't
just come from parents

00:02:01.030 --> 00:02:03.493
or from patients, it
also comes from doctors.

00:02:04.390 --> 00:02:06.500
What my advice is,

00:02:06.500 --> 00:02:08.700
is you know we're entering
a post antibiotic era.

00:02:08.700 --> 00:02:10.290
I mean, we have children in our hospital,

00:02:10.290 --> 00:02:13.390
especially some with say cystic
fibrosis, that are affected

00:02:13.390 --> 00:02:15.830
with bacteria that are
resistant to all commercially

00:02:15.830 --> 00:02:16.930
available antibiotics.

00:02:16.930 --> 00:02:19.380
I mean, we're starting to
use bacteria face therapy,

00:02:19.380 --> 00:02:22.480
meaning viruses that kill bacteria.

00:02:22.480 --> 00:02:24.900
I don't know if you ever read
Sinclair Lewis's Arrowsmith

00:02:24.900 --> 00:02:27.220
but I mean, that was
the subject of that book

00:02:27.220 --> 00:02:30.660
written in the early 1920s
before we had antibiotics.

00:02:30.660 --> 00:02:31.493
I mean,

00:02:31.493 --> 00:02:34.460
we used to use bacteria
phages a hundred years ago.

00:02:34.460 --> 00:02:37.010
It's a little frightening
that we're going back to that

00:02:37.010 --> 00:02:37.843
at some level.

00:02:37.843 --> 00:02:38.950
So that's, what's at stake.

00:02:38.950 --> 00:02:42.110
And so for me, the attitude
is I certainly understand

00:02:42.110 --> 00:02:43.120
where the parents are coming from

00:02:43.120 --> 00:02:44.240
or in the case of the hospital

00:02:44.240 --> 00:02:45.630
where the doctor's coming from.

00:02:45.630 --> 00:02:48.160
But we have a judicious
use of antibiotics program

00:02:48.160 --> 00:02:50.870
in our hospital and we push back.

00:02:50.870 --> 00:02:53.450
And I think you have to do the same thing,

00:02:53.450 --> 00:02:55.370
knowing that you will pay
a certain price for that,

00:02:55.370 --> 00:02:58.870
but the eyes on the prize
and the prize is the child.

00:02:58.870 --> 00:03:01.378
The prize is you wanna make
sure that you're not generating,

00:03:01.378 --> 00:03:03.230
more and more resistant bacteria,

00:03:03.230 --> 00:03:04.760
which will only be more and more difficult

00:03:04.760 --> 00:03:06.040
to treat in that child.

00:03:06.040 --> 00:03:08.220
So although the parent may want
it and may give you a better

00:03:08.220 --> 00:03:09.900
review, do the right thing.

00:03:09.900 --> 00:03:11.380
But as you know, doing the right thing,

00:03:11.380 --> 00:03:12.830
isn't always doing the easy thing.

00:03:12.830 --> 00:03:15.410
- The concept of medical
inertia you mentioned earlier,

00:03:15.410 --> 00:03:17.270
I think is so well stated in the book.

00:03:17.270 --> 00:03:19.290
I'd love for you to briefly touch on that.

00:03:19.290 --> 00:03:21.060
- Yeah, we do things a certain way

00:03:21.060 --> 00:03:22.800
and when you try and I know,

00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:24.880
cause I've given talks relating
to things in this book.

00:03:24.880 --> 00:03:27.350
And what you'll often hear
is push back from physicians

00:03:27.350 --> 00:03:28.620
is look, I mean,

00:03:28.620 --> 00:03:31.500
I've been doing it this way
for 20 years and it works well,

00:03:31.500 --> 00:03:33.333
but see, you don't know that
if you don't do it that way,

00:03:33.333 --> 00:03:35.110
that would also work, right?

00:03:35.110 --> 00:03:36.780
So that's what you're up against.

00:03:36.780 --> 00:03:39.710
You're up at this sort of anecdotal.

00:03:39.710 --> 00:03:41.880
I've done this and it's
worked and it's like,

00:03:41.880 --> 00:03:44.350
this is my experience,
but doing something wrong

00:03:44.350 --> 00:03:46.560
a thousand times doesn't
constitute experience.

00:03:46.560 --> 00:03:49.020
And also remember antibiotics
are not big money makers

00:03:49.020 --> 00:03:50.700
for these pharmaceutical companies,

00:03:50.700 --> 00:03:52.980
because there's something
you give just a few times say

00:03:52.980 --> 00:03:55.500
at most, during the year,
you're not taking it every day.

00:03:55.500 --> 00:03:57.380
They're never gonna compete
with lipid lowering agents

00:03:57.380 --> 00:03:59.118
or neurological drugs or diabetes drugs.

00:03:59.118 --> 00:04:01.380
So they're not a priority
for these companies.

00:04:01.380 --> 00:04:03.720
And we're there.

00:04:03.720 --> 00:04:06.120
We've taken our first steps
into the post antibiotic era.

00:04:06.120 --> 00:04:07.280
And I working in a hospital.

00:04:07.280 --> 00:04:09.080
I certainly see that
we've used bacteria phases

00:04:09.080 --> 00:04:11.490
in our hospital, on children
with cystic fibrosis

00:04:11.490 --> 00:04:13.640
who are infected with something that,

00:04:13.640 --> 00:04:15.070
for which we have no antibiotics,

00:04:15.070 --> 00:04:17.810
because we have pushed and
pushed and pushed that bacteria

00:04:17.810 --> 00:04:18.810
into complete resistance.

00:04:18.810 --> 00:04:21.040
The bacteria ultimately will win out.

00:04:21.040 --> 00:04:23.670
They will win out unless
we're careful about

00:04:23.670 --> 00:04:25.350
how we're handling all this.

00:04:25.350 --> 00:04:27.450
- Yeah, antibiotic stewardship
is really important

00:04:27.450 --> 00:04:28.860
and much in the same vein.

00:04:28.860 --> 00:04:31.240
One of the obstacles
I run into quite often

00:04:31.240 --> 00:04:33.650
is with fever reducing agents.

00:04:33.650 --> 00:04:36.270
Frequently, even amongst
friends and family,

00:04:36.270 --> 00:04:38.020
when they call me for advice,

00:04:38.020 --> 00:04:40.820
when I tell them to not
use fever lowering agents,

00:04:40.820 --> 00:04:42.821
when they have a very mild fever,

00:04:42.821 --> 00:04:44.350
there's shock on their face,

00:04:44.350 --> 00:04:47.296
because that is not something
that is generally talked

00:04:47.296 --> 00:04:50.740
about and I don't understand
why it's not taught

00:04:50.740 --> 00:04:51.740
in medical schools,

00:04:51.740 --> 00:04:53.860
it's not discussed amongst my colleagues.

00:04:53.860 --> 00:04:55.540
I've never had a lecture on it

00:04:55.540 --> 00:04:57.880
despite attending thousands of lectures.

00:04:57.880 --> 00:05:00.680
And yet there's so much
evidence that you stated in this

00:05:00.680 --> 00:05:05.510
book about allowing a fever to
help inflammatory cytokines,

00:05:05.510 --> 00:05:07.750
to help your immune
system function better,

00:05:07.750 --> 00:05:10.670
to potentially decrease autoimmune
conditions in the future.

00:05:10.670 --> 00:05:14.140
Why do we continue to
use fever lowering agents

00:05:14.140 --> 00:05:15.180
and what can we do about it?

00:05:15.180 --> 00:05:16.260
- Well, we continue to use them

00:05:16.260 --> 00:05:18.060
because they makes us feel better.

00:05:18.060 --> 00:05:20.933
It's a short lived gain,
but you're exactly right.

00:05:20.933 --> 00:05:22.490
I mean, you put your finger on it.

00:05:22.490 --> 00:05:23.680
Why don't we learn about this?

00:05:23.680 --> 00:05:26.220
I mean, I really learned about it the most

00:05:26.220 --> 00:05:28.360
when I read about it for the
purpose of writing the book.

00:05:28.360 --> 00:05:30.990
There are probably 20 studies
showing that for people

00:05:30.990 --> 00:05:33.170
who have let's say bacterial pneumonia

00:05:33.170 --> 00:05:34.603
that are treated with antibiotics,

00:05:34.603 --> 00:05:37.020
that they will have
either more severe disease

00:05:37.020 --> 00:05:38.230
or longer lasting disease.

00:05:38.230 --> 00:05:39.980
I mean, it's true over and
over again in the world

00:05:39.980 --> 00:05:41.600
of vaccines, which is my world.

00:05:41.600 --> 00:05:44.290
If you give antibiotics,
prior to getting a vaccine,

00:05:44.290 --> 00:05:47.210
you lower the immune response
because why do we make fever?

00:05:47.210 --> 00:05:49.470
It's not to keep pharmaceutical
companies in business.

00:05:49.470 --> 00:05:52.590
We make fever because our
immune system works better

00:05:52.590 --> 00:05:53.580
at a higher temperature.

00:05:53.580 --> 00:05:56.480
And across the board B
cells make antibodies

00:05:56.480 --> 00:05:59.210
sort of more efficiently
and at higher affinity

00:05:59.210 --> 00:06:00.200
and higher titer.

00:06:00.200 --> 00:06:01.760
T cells help B cells do it.

00:06:01.760 --> 00:06:04.767
Cytotoxic T cells help kill
viral infected cells that,

00:06:04.767 --> 00:06:06.260
but we pay a price for fever.

00:06:06.260 --> 00:06:08.200
You pay a metabolic price for fever,

00:06:08.200 --> 00:06:09.610
and it's uncomfortable having fever

00:06:09.610 --> 00:06:12.080
which is why we only have
fever when we need it.

00:06:12.080 --> 00:06:15.090
And so why blunt this critical aspect

00:06:15.090 --> 00:06:15.923
of your immune response?

00:06:15.923 --> 00:06:18.400
The one story I tell in the
book is it was so striking

00:06:18.400 --> 00:06:21.150
was a 15 year old boy
who was playing soccer.

00:06:21.150 --> 00:06:22.950
He's hitten in the hip with a soccer ball.

00:06:22.950 --> 00:06:27.300
He develops a thrombus, a
clot in his, in the vein

00:06:27.300 --> 00:06:28.160
at his hip.

00:06:28.160 --> 00:06:32.390
Which becomes infected with
the bacteria staph aureus

00:06:32.390 --> 00:06:33.620
which is resistant, right?

00:06:33.620 --> 00:06:35.240
So methicillin-resistant staph aureus.

00:06:35.240 --> 00:06:37.546
So we're treating with the
right antibiotic vancomycin

00:06:37.546 --> 00:06:39.180
day after day though,

00:06:39.180 --> 00:06:41.350
he continued to have bacteria
in his bloodstream because

00:06:41.350 --> 00:06:43.760
they had this septic thrombophlebitis,

00:06:43.760 --> 00:06:46.910
day after day after day,
continued to have high fever

00:06:46.910 --> 00:06:50.460
and was treated round the clock
with anti fever medicines.

00:06:50.460 --> 00:06:53.230
And after a while, I thought, you know,

00:06:53.230 --> 00:06:55.840
why blunt this critical
aspect of the mirror by?

00:06:55.840 --> 00:06:57.700
So I sat down with a boy who was brave.

00:06:57.700 --> 00:07:00.390
I sat down with his parents
who were perfectly willing

00:07:00.390 --> 00:07:02.070
to listen to what we had to say.

00:07:02.070 --> 00:07:04.780
And most importantly
sat down with the nurses

00:07:04.780 --> 00:07:07.660
because this is like one of
the pillars on which nursing

00:07:07.660 --> 00:07:09.750
stands is thermo regulation.

00:07:09.750 --> 00:07:11.300
So we all got in a room.

00:07:11.300 --> 00:07:14.520
We had this discussion and we
stopped treating his fever.

00:07:14.520 --> 00:07:17.370
And within two days his
blood culture cleared.

00:07:17.370 --> 00:07:18.240
We finally cleared it.

00:07:18.240 --> 00:07:20.250
Now that may have, that's
just the end of one.

00:07:20.250 --> 00:07:21.270
It may have been coincidence,

00:07:21.270 --> 00:07:23.850
but not as far as the parents
were concerned or the nurses.

00:07:23.850 --> 00:07:25.130
They thought we were geniuses

00:07:25.130 --> 00:07:27.390
because we just didn't
treat with antipyretic.

00:07:27.390 --> 00:07:30.060
- Yeah, I think electronic
medical records definitely plays

00:07:30.060 --> 00:07:32.560
a role here because it was so often that

00:07:32.560 --> 00:07:35.180
automatically when you
were treating an infectious

00:07:35.180 --> 00:07:36.610
disease patient,

00:07:36.610 --> 00:07:38.560
the orders almost automatically went in

00:07:38.560 --> 00:07:41.920
that if for fever above 101 give Tylenol,

00:07:41.920 --> 00:07:44.200
it was like one of the orders
sets that auto populated

00:07:44.200 --> 00:07:46.390
and I never understood
why that was the case.

00:07:46.390 --> 00:07:50.650
I even intuitively coming out
as a resident felt like that

00:07:50.650 --> 00:07:52.960
was wrong and I never
understood the logic.

00:07:52.960 --> 00:07:55.070
Obviously, if we have
a patient who's weak,

00:07:55.070 --> 00:07:58.090
who's not resting who has
cardiovascular risk, et cetera.

00:07:58.090 --> 00:08:00.100
There might be indications for it.

00:08:00.100 --> 00:08:01.520
But the whole majority of the population,

00:08:01.520 --> 00:08:02.500
I never understood.

00:08:02.500 --> 00:08:04.470
Even, I'm gonna throw
my father under the bus.

00:08:04.470 --> 00:08:08.070
He's a physician who is
trained in USSR as a physician,

00:08:08.070 --> 00:08:09.000
came to the United States,

00:08:09.000 --> 00:08:10.580
went to medical school here again.

00:08:10.580 --> 00:08:12.320
So that's two times medical school.

00:08:12.320 --> 00:08:13.830
And yet when I was younger,

00:08:13.830 --> 00:08:15.523
the second, my temperature went up,

00:08:15.523 --> 00:08:16.900
you have to take aspirin,

00:08:16.900 --> 00:08:18.140
you have to take Motrin something.

00:08:18.140 --> 00:08:20.950
And I would argue with
him, why am I taking this?

00:08:20.950 --> 00:08:22.810
Like, it didn't make sense to me.

00:08:22.810 --> 00:08:23.810
And to this day,

00:08:23.810 --> 00:08:26.390
he still believes treating
a fever is the right thing.

00:08:26.390 --> 00:08:30.270
Because of, I think the concept
that you so well illuminate,

00:08:30.270 --> 00:08:31.380
which is medical inertia.

00:08:31.380 --> 00:08:33.550
It worked for him for
so long, keep doing it.

00:08:33.550 --> 00:08:34.880
Why change what works?

00:08:34.880 --> 00:08:36.450
Back to the interview in just a second.

